    <summaries>
      <summary>
        <versionCode>00</versionCode>
        <actionDate>2026-03-16</actionDate>
        <actionDesc>Introduced in House</actionDesc>
        <updateDate>2026-11-12T20:35:23Z</updateDate>
        <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Settlement Agreement Information Database Act of 2026</strong></p><p>This bill requires federal agencies to publish a searchable, online database of information about certain settlement agreements entered into by the agency. The database must include</p><ul><li>a copy of each settlement agreement,</li><li>whether the agreement resolves a civil or criminal claim (or both),</li><li>the date the agreement was executed,</li><li>any alleged statutory violations,</li><li>the amount of attorneys fees provided by or to the United States,</li><li>any payment made to or by the federal government,</li><li>the projected duration of the agreement,&nbsp;and</li><li>any unit of government that is identified by the terms of the agreement.</li></ul><p>In general, the bill applies to settlement agreements that</p><ul><li>require at least $10 million in payments by a settling party;</li><li>involve the appointment of a special master or monitor;</li><li>involve a party that is a state, county, or local government; or</li><li>meet other criteria as determined by the Office of Management and Budget (OMB).</li></ul><p>The bill excludes several types of settlement agreements from the database, including agreements related to a federal employee personnel action, a non-prosecution agreement or plea bargain,&nbsp;or the Internal Revenue Code.</p><p>To extent practicable, the bill also applies retroactively to covered settlement agreements that were&nbsp;entered into on or after January 1, 2015,&nbsp;and remain in effect on or after the date of enactment.</p><p>The OMB must issue guidance to implement the bill.</p>]]></text>
      </summary>
    </summaries>
